#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""check-ci-supply-chain - G56 CI supply-chain config hygiene (P0.2.6 + P0.2.7 runner-host
+ P0.2.10 cargo-fuzz toolchain pin).

The residual CI/config assertions that zizmor (G50) + actionlint (G49) do NOT cover.
STDLIB-ONLY (no YAML library - the gate plane forbids vendoring one). A naive line scan
is trivially evaded by valid-but-exotic YAML dialects, so this parser is HARDENED and -
crucially - FAIL-CLOSED: anything it cannot statically reason about (a YAML construct a
real parser resolves differently than a line scan) makes it ERROR rather than pass
vacuously. zizmor remains the PRIMARY YAML-parsing G50 control; this is the G56 residual
backstop against a future malicious/careless L(-1) `.github/**` diff.

Fail-closed pre-pass (every workflow + dependabot.yml the gate reasons about) - the DIALECT every
structural reader below relies on (precheck; the r5-r9 reviews closed it refusal by refusal):
  * multiple YAML documents (`---` / `...` beyond a leading marker) -> reject; a UTF-8 BOM anywhere
    -> reject (at the file start YAML strips it and a line scan would read `\ufeffjobs` as a key
    and skip every job-level check; at column 0 of a later line libyaml / yaml.v3 skip it while
    YamlDotNet / PyYAML / js-yaml keep it; G52 forbids the BOM anyway);
  * a quoted mapping key on a structural line -> reject: the dialect reads plain keys only, so every
    reader matches ONE spelling (a quoted key hid `use-policy-store` from the harden-runner read and
    `uses` from the engine-cache restore read - the r14 / r15 reviews; 0 live);
  * a character Python treats as WHITE SPACE or a LINE BREAK outside the four every YAML parser
    agrees on -> reject, anywhere in the file: YAML's white space is the space and the tab and its
    line breaks are LF and CR,
    while Python's str.isspace() / re `\\s` / str.strip() / str.splitlines() - the primitives every
    reader here is built on - also see VT / FF / FS / GS / RS / U+001F / NEL / U+00A0 / U+1680 /
    U+2000-U+200A / U+2028 / U+2029 / U+202F / U+205F / U+3000 (25 characters). Text after one of
    the break members inside a comment is a real line to a YAML 1.1 parser (GitHub's YamlDotNet
    workflow parser, libyaml / PyYAML, actionlint's yaml.v3 break at NEL / U+2028 / U+2029; the
    ASCII members are rejected by libyaml / PyYAML / yaml.v3 and read as content by YamlDotNet)
    and comment to an LF reader (the r9-r11 reviews' forged block-scalar header); a key or an
    indent spelled with a space member is a different key or indent to YAML than to a `\\s` regex
    (the r12 review's `package-ecosystem:<U+00A0>"npm"`, complete to the gate, unparseable to
    Dependabot). The predicate is Python's own (`c not in ' \t\r\n' and c.isspace()`), applied to
    the file's characters and pinned over every code point by a self-test leg, so no member can
    be missed (an enumerated list missed VT once) and every accepted file splits, indents and
    keys identically to every parser family;
  * every structural line (comment-stripped, block-scalar bodies excised) is a `key:` entry, a
    `- ` item, a bare `-` or a document marker, nothing else -> a line led by `?` / `:` / any
    other indicator, a plain-scalar continuation line and a flow collection at the start of a
    line's content are rejected, so a key and its `:` always share a line; a quoted node that
    runs off its line -> reject (the value or key would continue on a line this reader never joins);
  * any backslash on a structural line -> reject (no escape model: an escape can spell any key
    or value and desynchronise the expression pre-pass);
  * YAML anchors (`&name`), aliases (`*name`), merge keys (`<<:`), explicit tags (`!!type` /
    `!tag`) and explicit keys in flow position (`{ ? k : v }`) -> reject, detected at YAML
    node-start positions only, so a `*glob` / `&bg` shell token mid-scalar in a single-line
    `run:` is not a false anchor;
  * a flow collection spanning lines, an inline `jobs:` value, any inline job-id value and a
    `steps` key followed by a flow opener anywhere on a line -> reject (a step GitHub reads is
    then a block item under a block `steps:` under a block job under a block `jobs:`); an inline
    FLOW-MAPPING value under the other security-relevant keys (`on:` and its children,
    `permissions:` at top-level AND per-job) -> reject, a flow map hides key/value structure
    from a line scan (a flow value inline after any other key, `branches: [main]`, is read);
  * duplicate TOP-LEVEL mapping keys -> reject (YAML resolves a dup to the LAST, a line scan
    would trust the FIRST);
  * block-scalar bodies (`run: |` / `: >`) are excised before any structural scan so literal
    shell text never masquerades as - or hides - YAML structure.

Assertions (after the file is known to be in plain, statically-analysable block form):
  (1) .github/dependabot.yml exists + declares all four ecosystems (github-actions,
      cargo, npm, pip), each item carrying a `directory` and a `schedule` at the item's
      own child indent (zizmor checks a PRESENT file's content, never its presence).
  (2) id-token discipline - no `id-token:` permission key anywhere (top-level OR per-job),
      in block, quoted-key (`'id-token':`) or flow (`{ id-token: write }`, via the pre-pass)
      form (the release-job `id-token: write` lands in P10; until then it is forbidden).
  (3) concurrency POLARITY - a push-(non-release)-triggered workflow declares
      `cancel-in-progress: true` (any YAML-1.1 truthy spelling: true/True/yes/on); a
      RELEASE-CLASS workflow (push.tags / release / create) must NOT (a cancelled release
      is worse than a delayed one; release-safety dominates when a workflow is both, and a
      `${{ }}` cancel-in-progress expression cannot prove it won't cancel -> fail-closed for
      a release). The trigger is classified from ONLY the first indentation level under `on:`
      (so a deep `workflow_dispatch.inputs.tags` / a valid `schedule: - cron:` is not
      mistaken for a trigger); an `on:` form that cannot be classified (flow-style / alias /
      block-list-of-mappings / unknown) -> fail-closed.
  (4) per-JOB `timeout-minutes` at the job's own child indent (a step-level timeout does
      not count); flow-style job mappings are rejected fail-closed; a reusable-workflow
      call job (`uses:` with a `.github/` path or `@ref` value) is exempt (GitHub forbids
      job-level timeout-minutes there) - a non-ref `uses:` value buys no exemption.
  (5) permissions read-only - TOP-LEVEL must exist with `contents: read`; NO permissions
      block (top-level OR per-job - GitHub per-job permissions OVERRIDE the default) may set
      any key to write/write-all (until the P10 release job); a value that a line scan cannot
      resolve (empty/next-line or a block-scalar `|`/`>` indicator), a flow-style block, and
      a non-`read-all` inline scalar are all rejected fail-closed.
  (6) runner-host integrity (P0.2.7, security-concept principle 11) - a job that reads a CI
      secret (`${{ secrets.* }}`) must NOT run on a self-hosted runner, and a GitHub-hosted
      secret job must use step-security/harden-runner with `egress-policy: block` (read to the
      value's end, balanced-quoted, case-sensitive; a quoted key refused by the dialect; a step carrying
      `use-policy-store` / `policy:` / `denied-endpoints` does not count - the dashboard policy or
      the deny-list mode replaces the pinned block); a secret
      job whose runs-on is not a literal recognized hosted image is rejected fail-closed.
      Non-secret jobs are unconstrained (fail-open until the P10 signing job lands).
  (7) cargo-fuzz toolchain pin (P0.2.10, G37) - every `cargo ... fuzz` invocation names the
      DATE-PINNED nightly channel (`cargo +nightly-YYYY-MM-DD fuzz`), never a bare/implicit
      +nightly (which would float off the pinned toolchain). Fail-open until a fuzz job lands.
  (8) build.rs/proc-macro execution-isolation for the secret job (P0.4.4, §3.8) - a secret-reading
      job that runs a COMPILING command (cargo build/test/run/bench/check/install/doc/publish/package/
      clippy/rustc/miri, OR a `tauri build` wrapper - each executes build.rs + proc-macros = arbitrary
      native code) must set `CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true` AND carry an explicit `cargo fetch --locked`
      BEFORE the build, so a build script cannot phone home to exfiltrate MINISIGN_SECRET_KEY (cargo-vet
      is a trust signal, not an execution sandbox; harden-runner BLOCK in (6) is the network backstop;
      the per-crate cap is the P0.4.5 cargo-acl upgrade). The compile scan is scoped to the `steps:`
      section (a job-level env value embedding "cargo build" is not a phantom compile). Fail-open until
      the P10 signing job lands. Spec §6.7.2 makes the secret-bearing signing job sign-only + disjoint
      from the build job, so this binds only if that disjoint-job design is ever violated.
  (9) container/service image digests (the G56 r6 image-digest sub-rule; its target arrived
      with the P4.28.1 engine-asset-compile container) - every `container:` scalar and every
      `image:` value must be the digest-pinned `<registry>/<image>[:tag]@sha256:<64-hex>`
      form; a mutable tag, an expression, a flow-style `container: {...}`, a value-less
      `image:` (next-line/block value) and a `container:` block with no resolvable `image:`
      child all fail closed (zizmor G50 pins ACTION SHAs but not image digests). The scan is
      deliberately WIDER than jobs.<job>.container/services: it matches ANY `image:`/
      `container:` key at any depth, so a future legitimate non-container `image:` key (a
      matrix dimension, a workflow_dispatch input) lands as a documented exemption, never a
      silent pass. The r6 sentence's transitive-action-pin half is the owner act P4.97 (fetch every
      referenced action's action.y*ml at its pinned SHA, then check nested uses: pins and docker
      runs.image digests over the copies; pinact - which resolves tags to SHAs and version comments
      through the GitHub API, reads the local workflow and action files it is handed, and never
      reads a referenced REMOTE action's `action.y*ml`; its `--no-api` mode errors on a bare SHA -
      and the reserved G67 Scorecard leg corroborate only), not this line scan.
 (10) the G37 restore-re-verify ordering (the G56 workflow-lint sub-rule the G37 row names) -
      a JOB that restores an engine-asset cache (`actions/cache*` + a `.engine-cache` path)
      may not also invoke `scripts/stage-engines`: no lint-recognizable SHA-256 re-verify
      actor exists between them, so the pair fails CLOSED. Content is scanned over the RAW
      (comment-stripped, NOT block-scalar-excised) job bodies - the check-(8) dual-view
      precedent, because a `run: |` body is exactly where a stage-engines invocation lives.
      The owner act P10.62 extends this rule with the sanctioned verify step (and the planted-
      mismatch self-test the G37 row asks for); P10.9, the box that wires staging, needs it.
      Restore-only jobs (the populate/compile pair) and stage-engines-without-restore jobs (the
      check-mode pass) are the sanctioned shapes.
 (11) actions/cache LOCKSTEP (2026-09-24) - every `uses:` of `actions/cache`, `actions/cache/save`
      or `actions/cache/restore` across ALL workflows pins ONE lowercase 40-hex sha: one action
      version writes AND reads a cache entry (Dependabot offers the halves as two PRs - #30 bumped
      save only). Read over the structural view (comment-stripped, block-scalar bodies excised: a
      `run: |` body is a string GitHub reads no step from). The `uses` SHAPE rule closes the class
      instead of listing odd forms: every `uses` key token on a structural line must be the key of
      a plain block step line `[- ]uses: <value>` with the value on the same line and no block-scalar
      indicator - a next-line or block-scalar value, a second key token on the line and a `uses` key
      in any other position fail CLOSED. The rule is sound because the precheck restricts the YAML
      DIALECT a structural line may use (the r7-r8 reviews: a step written as a flow collection in a
      spelling the tokenizer's node model did not reproduce - a ` - ` inside a flow scalar, an
      expression end hidden behind an escaped `\x7d`, a flow mapping on the line after a bare `-`
      or after `steps:`, an inline `jobs: {...}` - carried a `uses` key the shape rule never saw).
      Refused, each loud and fail-closed: every structural line must be a `key:` entry or a `- `
      item (the line SHAPE - a line led by `?` / `:` / any other indicator, a plain-scalar
      continuation line and a flow collection at the start of a line's content are refused, so a
      key and its `:` always share a line and a sequence item is never a flow collection; the r9
      review's `?` at a line end with the key on the next line); ANY backslash on a structural
      line (a YAML escape can spell any key or value and desynchronise the expression scan, so the
      dialect has no escapes and a double-quoted node simply ends at its next `"`); a quoted mapping
      key (the dialect reads plain keys only, so every reader matches one spelling - a quoted key hid
      `use-policy-store` from the harden-runner read and `uses` from the restore read); an inline
      value on the top-level `jobs:` line and on any job-id line (parse_jobs); a `steps` key
      followed by a flow collection anywhere on a line; a flow collection spanning lines; a quoted
      node running off its line; explicit keys in flow position, anchors and aliases of any YAML
      name, merge keys and tags; a BOM anywhere; any character Python treats as white space or a line
      break outside the four every YAML parser agrees on (25 of them - the 8 str.splitlines() breaks,
      NEL / U+2028 / U+2029 being breaks to GitHub's YAML 1.1 parser too, and the 17 further
      `isspace()` members such as U+00A0; the predicate is Python's own, applied to the file's
      characters, pinned over every code point by a self-test leg, the refusal driven per character),
      so every accepted file splits, indents and keys identically to every
      parser and to this reader (a flow value inline after a key that is not `jobs`, a job id or
      `steps`, `branches: [main]` / `with: {path: x}`, is read).
      Under that dialect a step GitHub reads is a block sequence item under a block `steps:` under
      a block job under a block `jobs:`, so its `uses` key can only sit on a block line - the
      reader never reconstructs a flow item from YAML's quote / indicator grammar, and the two
      premises hold by construction (the r5/r6 reviews): a block scalar is excised from its
      header's KEY column with the indicator after whitespace (a folded `- name: >-` never
      swallows the step's own `uses:`; `save:>` is no header). The structural scanners share one
      node-aware view (_tokenize: the comment cut, the quote blanking, the unterminated scan, the
      flow depth), so an apostrophe inside a plain scalar hides nothing anywhere; the RAW view that
      checks (6), (8), (10) and the fuzz pin read over `run: |` bodies keeps the pre-existing
      shell-quote-aware cut, because a body line is shell, not YAML (the r7 review's P0). A
      plainly quoted value is read through its quotes (a quoted key is refused); the owner/repo is matched
      case-insensitively and token-bounded (a local `./.github/actions/cache-engines` is not the
      cache action); a resolved cache `uses:` without a lowercase 40-hex sha fails closed.
      Named residuals, one per refusal of the dialect (loud, fail-closed): a `uses:` spelled inside a
      `name:` / `if:` / `with:` value reds (reword it); any structural line outside the shape reds - a
      line led by `?` / `:` / another indicator (a block-scalar header written on the line AFTER its key,
      `run:` / `|`, is such a line: write `run: |`), a plain-scalar continuation line (write the value
      on one line or as a block scalar), a flow collection at the start of a line's content, any
      sequence item written as a flow collection (a step, a matrix `include:` entry, a dependabot
      list item); a flow value spanning lines reds (write it on one line); an inline `jobs:` or
      job-id value reds (write block style); a `steps` token followed by a flow opener anywhere on a
      raw structural line reds, even inside a quoted scalar (`- name: 'steps: [a]'` - reword it); a
      backslash anywhere on a structural line reds, quoted or not (single quotes clear only an
      escaped quote, `"a \"b\""` -> `'a "b"'`; a literal backslash needs a block scalar); a quoted
      node running off its line reds (an escaped line break is a backslash anyway); a BOM anywhere
      reds (G52 forbids it too); a character Python treats as white space or a line break outside the
      four every YAML parser agrees on reds anywhere in the file (remove it); an anchor, alias, merge
      key or tag, an explicit key in flow position, a quoted mapping key (write it plainly), a second
      document, a duplicate top-level key and a flow map under `on:` / `permissions:` red
      (write plain block style). The caged-file review (G71 + G1) is the backstop behind every
      structural refusal.

fail-closed: any violation -> exit 1. L(-1) security-critical (scripts/l-neg1-files.toml, G71).

Usage: check-ci-supply-chain [--root DIR]
Exit:  0 = clean; 1 = a violation; 2 = .github not found under --root.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import re
import sys
import tomllib
from pathlib import Path
for _stream in (sys.stdout, sys.stderr):          # the console's codepage is not this script's concern (G9 invariant i)
    if hasattr(_stream, "reconfigure"):
        _stream.reconfigure(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")

REQUIRED_ECOSYSTEMS = {"github-actions", "cargo", "npm", "pip"}
# a GitHub-HOSTED runner image label (incl. -arm / -large / -N-core variants); the only
# safe host for a secret-bearing job. Anything else - self-hosted, a custom label, a
# matrix/${{ }} expression, an object runs-on - is rejected fail-closed. (A self-hosted
# runner CAN be mislabelled to look hosted; that residual is zizmor/the P10 review's job.)
HOSTED_RUNNER = re.compile(r"^(ubuntu|macos|windows)-[\w.-]+$", re.I)
# the `secrets` CONTEXT word, used by reads_secret() inside a ${{ }} expression interior
# (a literal `secrets.txt` run-string has no ${{; `inputs.secrets_x` has no word boundary).
SECRETS_WORD = re.compile(r"\bsecrets\b", re.I)


def fail(msg: str) -> None:
    print(f"[G56] FAIL: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)


def _blank_expressions(line: str) -> str:
    """(pure) Every `${{ ... }}` GitHub expression blanked to spaces, its end found by a scan that honours the
    expression's own single-quoted strings (`''` is the escape) - a `}}` inside such a string, as in
    `${{ toJSON(fromJSON('{"a":{"b":1}}')) }}`, does not end it (the r7 review's non-greedy defect). An
    expression that never closes on the line is blanked to the end of the line."""
    out: list[str] = []
    i, n = 0, len(line)
    while i < n:
        if line.startswith("${{", i):
            j = i + 3
            in_str = False
            end = None
            while j < n:
                ch = line[j]
                if in_str:
                    if ch == "'":
                        if j + 1 < n and line[j + 1] == "'":
                            j += 2
                            continue
                        in_str = False
                elif ch == "'":
                    in_str = True
                elif line.startswith("}}", j):
                    end = j + 2
                    break
                j += 1
            end = n if end is None else end
            out.append(" " * (end - i))
            i = end
            continue
        out.append(line[i])
        i += 1
    return "".join(out)


def _tokenize(line: str) -> tuple[str, str, bool, int, int]:
    """(pure) ONE YAML-node-aware pass over a STRUCTURAL line - the single quote model every structural scanner
    in this gate shares (the r6 review: three scanners with two quote models let a mid-scalar apostrophe in a
    flow scalar hide a flow-position `? "u\x73es"` key from the precheck). A quote opens a quoted node only at a
    node start; a node starts at the line start, after a `- ` or `? ` indicator that itself stands at a node
    start (a ` - ` inside a plain scalar starts nothing - the r7 review's fake node), after `: ` anywhere, after
    a quoted key's `:` even with whitespace before it (`"k" : "v"`, the JSON-like form, in any context), and
    after a flow `{` / `[` / `,`. `{` / `[` open a flow collection only at a node start (a brace inside a
    plain scalar is content); `}` / `]` close one only while one is open. A double-quoted node ends at its
    next `"` - the precheck refuses every backslash on a structural line, so this reader carries no escape
    model; inside a single-quoted node `''` is the escape. A `#` preceded by
    whitespace outside a quoted node starts the comment. `${{ ... }}` expressions are blanked first
    (_blank_expressions). Returns (the line with every quoted node and expression blanked to spaces, the line
    cut at its comment, whether a quoted node runs off the line, the flow depth still open at the end of the
    line, the count of quoted KEYS - a quoted node followed by `:` at any node start, block or flow position,
    which the dialect refuses: the r16 review's `concurrency: {group: rel-x, "cancel-in-progress": true}`)."""
    scan = _blank_expressions(line)
    out: list[str] = []
    i, n = 0, len(scan)
    at_node_start = True
    flow_depth = 0
    comment_at: int | None = None
    unterminated = False
    quoted_keys = 0
    while i < n:
        ch = scan[i]
        if ch in "\"'" and at_node_start:
            q, start = ch, i
            i += 1
            while i < n:
                if scan[i] == q:
                    if q == "'" and i + 1 < n and scan[i + 1] == "'":
                        i += 2
                        continue
                    break
                i += 1
            else:
                unterminated = True
                out.append(" " * (n - start))
                break
            i += 1
            out.append(" " * (i - start))
            k = i
            while k < n and scan[k] in " \t":
                k += 1
            if k < n and scan[k] == ":":
                out.append(scan[i:k] + ":")
                i = k + 1
                at_node_start = True
                quoted_keys += 1
            else:
                at_node_start = False
            continue
        if ch == "#" and (i == 0 or scan[i - 1] in " \t"):
            comment_at = i
            break
        out.append(ch)
        if ch in " \t":
            i += 1
            continue
        if ch in "{[" and at_node_start:
            flow_depth += 1
        elif ch in "}]" and flow_depth:
            flow_depth -= 1
            at_node_start = False
        elif ch == "," and flow_depth:
            at_node_start = True
        elif ch in "-?" and at_node_start and (i + 1 >= n or scan[i + 1] in " \t"):
            at_node_start = True
        elif ch == ":" and (i + 1 >= n or scan[i + 1] in " \t"):
            at_node_start = True
        else:
            at_node_start = False
        i += 1
    blanked = "".join(out)
    stripped = (line if comment_at is None else line[:comment_at]).rstrip()
    return blanked, stripped, unterminated, flow_depth, quoted_keys


def _quoted_key(line: str) -> bool:
    """(pure) True when the structural line carries a quoted mapping key at any node start - block or flow
    position (the tokenizer's verdict; a quoted VALUE, a quoted ITEM and a quoted string inside a quoted value are
    not keys). A `''`-escaped single-quoted key is a quoted key too; the line shape refuses it as well."""
    return _tokenize(line)[4] > 0


def strip_comment(line: str) -> str:
    """Drop a trailing YAML `#` comment from a RAW-view line, respecting single/double quotes wherever they
    sit - the pre-2026-09-24 cut, kept for the raw view on purpose: checks (6), (8), (10) and the fuzz pin read
    block-scalar BODY lines, which are shell, and a shell-quoted `"a #"` must keep the rest of its line (the
    r7 review: the node-aware cut opened no mid-line quote there and hid a secret read, a compile and a
    stage-engines call). The structural view uses strip_comment_structural."""
    q = None
    for i, ch in enumerate(line):
        if q:
            if ch == q:
                q = None
        elif ch in "\"'":
            q = ch
        elif ch == "#" and (i == 0 or line[i - 1] in " \t"):
            return line[:i].rstrip()
    return line.rstrip()


def strip_comment_structural(line: str) -> str:
    """Drop a trailing YAML `#` comment from a STRUCTURAL line: a `#` after whitespace outside a quoted NODE
    (the tokenizer's cut - `echo it's # x` loses its comment; `'a # b'` keeps its content)."""
    return _tokenize(line)[1]


def unquote(s: str) -> str:
    s = s.strip()
    if len(s) >= 2 and s[0] == s[-1] and s[0] in "\"'":
        return s[1:-1]
    return s


def blank_spans(line: str) -> str:
    """Blank out `${{ ... }}` expressions and every quoted NODE of a structural line (so anchor/alias/tag/
    explicit-key scans on the result cannot false-positive on `&&` in an expression or a `*` glob in a quote,
    and cannot be HIDDEN by an apostrophe inside a plain scalar - the tokenizer's view)."""
    return _tokenize(line)[0]


def _unterminated_quoted_scalar(line: str) -> bool:
    """(pure) True when a quoted node opened on this structural line is not closed on it: the scalar continues
    on the next line, which this reader never joins (the tokenizer's verdict)."""
    return _tokenize(line)[2]


def _open_flow_depth(line: str) -> int:
    """(pure) The number of flow collections a structural line opens and does not close - non-zero means the
    collection spans lines (the tokenizer's count: braces inside plain scalars are content, not indicators)."""
    return _tokenize(line)[3]


def _block_scalar_indent(stripped: str) -> int | None:
    """(pure) The parent indentation of a block-scalar header - the column every content line must EXCEED:
    the KEY column after any `- ` prefixes for a mapping value (`key: |`, `- key: >-`, `- - key: |`), the
    LAST dash column for a sequence item that is itself the scalar (`- |`). None when the line is no
    block-scalar header. The indicator must follow whitespace: `key:|` is a plain scalar, and so is a
    value ending in `:>` (`- name: save:>`) - the r5 review's two mis-read headers. Measured at the dash
    column, a folded `- name: >-` swallowed the step's own `uses:` and `with:` siblings."""
    m = re.match(r"^(\s*(?:-\s+)*)(.*)$", stripped)
    prefix, rest = m.group(1), m.group(2)
    if re.fullmatch(r"[|>][+\-0-9]*", rest) and prefix.rstrip().endswith("-"):
        return len(prefix.rstrip()) - 1
    if not rest.startswith(("{", "[")) and re.search(r":\s+[|>][+\-0-9]*$", rest):
        return len(prefix)
    return None


def structural_lines(raw: list[str]) -> list[str]:
    """Comment-strip every line and excise block-scalar (`|`/`>`) bodies, so what
    remains is the file's analysable YAML structure only. A body line is one indented
    MORE than the header's parent column (_block_scalar_indent); the first non-blank
    line at or below that column ends the body."""
    stripped = [strip_comment_structural(ln) for ln in raw]
    keep = [True] * len(stripped)
    i = 0
    while i < len(stripped):
        indent = _block_scalar_indent(stripped[i])
        if indent is not None:
            j = i + 1
            while j < len(stripped):
                b = stripped[j]
                if not b.strip():
                    keep[j] = False
                    j += 1
                    continue
                if (len(b) - len(b.lstrip())) > indent:
                    keep[j] = False
                    j += 1
                else:
                    break
            i = j
            continue
        i += 1
    return [s for s, k in zip(stripped, keep) if k]


# a character Python treats as WHITE SPACE or a LINE BREAK outside the four every YAML parser agrees on: YAML's white
# space is the space and the tab, its line breaks LF and CR; Python's str.isspace() / re `\s` / str.strip() /
# str.splitlines() - the primitives every reader here is built on - also see 25 other characters. The break members
# hid a forged block-scalar header behind a comment (the r9-r11 reviews: a YAML 1.1 parser - GitHub's YamlDotNet
# workflow parser, libyaml / PyYAML, yaml.v3 - breaks at NEL / U+2028 / U+2029; libyaml / PyYAML / yaml.v3 reject the
# ASCII members and YamlDotNet reads them as content; str.splitlines() breaks at all eight); the space members spell a
# different key or indent to a `\s` regex than to YAML (the r12 review: `package-ecosystem:<U+00A0>"npm"` counts as an
# ecosystem here and fails to parse in Dependabot; a U+00A0-indented `services:` entry is excised as a block-scalar
# body). The predicate is Python's own, applied to the file's characters (never an enumerated list - one missed VT),
# and the self-test pins it over every code point (25 characters, the 8 splitlines breaks among them) and drives the
# refusal itself with each of them. Live workflows + dependabot.yml: 0.
def _foreign_space(c: str) -> bool:
    """(pure) True for a character Python treats as white space or a line break outside the four every YAML
    parser agrees on (space, tab, LF, CR)."""
    return c not in " \t\r\n" and c.isspace()


def foreign_spaces(name: str, text: str) -> int:
    """1 (and a failure) when the raw file carries such a character; else 0."""
    found = sorted(c for c in set(text) if _foreign_space(c))
    if not found:
        return 0
    fail(f"{name}: carries U+{ord(found[0]):04X}, a character Python treats as whitespace or a line break outside the "
         "four every YAML parser agrees on (space, tab, LF, CR) - text after it inside a comment would be a real line "
         "to one parser family, a key or an indent spelled with it a different key or indent to another; remove it")
    return 1


# a UTF-8 BOM anywhere: at the file start YAML strips it, so a line scan that kept it would read `\ufeffjobs` as a
# key and skip every job-level check (the r10 review's pre-existing form); at column 0 of a LATER line libyaml and
# yaml.v3 skip it (and count one column) while YamlDotNet, PyYAML, js-yaml and this reader keep it as content, so
# `<U+FEFF>FOO: bar` is a nested key to one family and a top-level key to the other (the r13 review). G52 forbids
# the BOM on every text file; this reader refuses it anywhere rather than silently mis-reading.
def utf8_bom(name: str, text: str) -> int:
    """1 (and a failure) when the raw file carries U+FEFF anywhere; else 0."""
    at = text.find("\ufeff")
    if at < 0:
        return 0
    fail(f"{name}: carries a UTF-8 BOM (U+FEFF) at offset {at} - at the file start YAML strips it and a line scan "
         "would read the first key with it, at column 0 of a later line libyaml skips it while YamlDotNet keeps it; "
         "G52 forbids "
         "the BOM (charset utf-8); remove it")
    return 1


# the line SHAPE of the dialect: after any `- ` prefixes a structural line is a `key:` entry (a plain key that
# starts with no indicator and carries no `:` / `#` - a quoted key is outside the shape AND refused by the
# quoted-key arm with its own message, so the shape
# carries no quoted-key alternative (the r16 review: a dead model); the `:` followed by
# whitespace or the line end; any value on the same line), a scalar item (after `- ` only: plain and not led by an
# indicator, or quoted), a block-scalar item `- |` / `- >-`, or a bare `-`. Nothing else: a line led by `?` (the
# key would sit on the next line - the r9 review), by `:` (the key sat on the previous line), by any other
# indicator, a plain-scalar continuation line and a flow collection at the start of a line's content are all
# refused, so a key and its `:` always share a line and a sequence item is never a flow collection (the r7-r8
# forms); a document marker (`---` / `...`) is admitted and left to the multi-document check. Measured
# 2026-09-24: 495 of 495 live structural lines match. The mutant battery shows which clauses are controls and
# which are defence in depth (conservative, unpinned because another rule already catches the hiding form):
# the `?` exclusion (a `?` alone on a line hides nothing by itself - the key line after it is refused by the
# continuation / `:` arm), a `:` inside a plain key (a `key:value` without the space is refused by the colon
# rule), and a `#` inside one (`a#b: c` keeps its `#` through the cut - the gate and YAML read the same key).
# The dash arm is strict: a run of dashes
# separated by whitespace ending in a bare `-`,
# so `--` is a refused continuation line.
_SHAPE_KEY = r"(?:[^\s\-?:,\[\]{}#&*!|>'\"%@`][^:#]*?)"
_SHAPE_ITEM = r"(?:'[^'\\]*'|\"[^\"\\]*\"|[^\s\-?:,\[\]{}#&*!|>'\"%@`].*|[|>][+\-0-9]*)"
_LINE_SHAPE_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:(?:-\s+)*" + _SHAPE_KEY + r"\s*:(?:\s.*|$)|(?:-\s+)+" + _SHAPE_ITEM + r"|(?:-\s+)*-|---|\.\.\.)$")


def _shape_verdict(ln: str) -> str:
    """(pure) Why a structural line is outside the dialect's shape - the first character of its content decides."""
    content = re.sub(r"^\s*(?:-\s+)*", "", ln)
    head = content[:1]
    if head in "{[":
        return ("a flow collection at the start of a line's content (a sequence item written as a flow collection, "
                "or a flow value placed on the line after its key)")
    if head == "?":
        return "an explicit-key indicator `?` leading the line (the key would sit on the next line)"
    if head == ":":
        return "an explicit-value indicator `:` leading the line (the key sat on the previous line)"
    if head and head in "&*!|>%@`,]}":
        return f"a YAML indicator `{head}` leading the line"
    if head and head in "\"'":
        return "a quoted mapping key - write it plainly (the dialect reads plain keys only)"
    return "a plain-scalar continuation line, or a key without its `:` on the same line"


def precheck(name: str, lines: list[str]) -> int:
    """Fail-closed on YAML constructs a stdlib line scan cannot safely resolve - the dialect (module docstring)."""
    problems = 0

    # multiple documents
    seen_content, markers = False, 0
    for ln in lines:
        st = ln.strip()
        if not st:
            continue
        if st == "---" or st.startswith("--- "):
            markers += 1
            if seen_content or markers > 1:
                fail(f"{name}: multiple YAML documents (--- / ...) - the gate verifies a single document")
                problems += 1
                break
            continue
        if st == "..." or st.startswith("... "):
            fail(f"{name}: YAML document-end marker (...) - the gate verifies a single document")
            problems += 1
            break
        seen_content = True

    # anchors / aliases / merge keys / tags / explicit keys - detected ONLY at YAML node-start positions
    # (after `:`+space, after one or more `- ` sequence prefixes, at a flow `[`/`{`/`,`, or at the line
    # start), so a shell token like `cp dist/ *staging` or `echo $X &bg` in a single-line `run:` scalar -
    # where `*`/`&` sit mid-scalar, not at a node start - is NOT misread as YAML structure. An anchor or
    # alias name is any run of non-space, non-flow-indicator characters (`&-c`, `*.x` included - the r5
    # review's evasion of an alphanumeric class). A flow collection opened on one line and closed on
    # another (the tokenizer's open depth at the end of the line - braces inside plain scalars are
    # content) is refused too: a key on its continuation line is not resolvable by a line reader (the r5
    # review's `- {` / `uses:` / `, with:`).
    flagged = set()
    node_start = r"(?::\s+|^\s*(?:-\s+)*|[\[{,]\s*)"
    for ln in lines:
        if ln.strip() and not _LINE_SHAPE_RE.match(ln) and "shape" not in flagged:
            fail(f"{name}: a structural line outside the dialect's shape (`{ln.strip()[:60]}`): {_shape_verdict(ln)} - "
                 "a structural line is a `key:` entry or a `- ` item; write block style, one key or item per line, "
                 "a long value as a block scalar")
            problems += 1
            flagged.add("shape")
        # a quoted node that runs off its line continues on a line this reader never joins - a key or value
        # spelled across lines (the r10 review's dependabot form: a quoted `prefix: "deps(cargo)` swallowing the
        # next entry's lines, which YAML folds into the string while check (1) read a phantom ecosystem).
        if _unterminated_quoted_scalar(ln) and "unterminated" not in flagged:
            fail(f"{name}: an unterminated quoted node (`{ln.strip()[:60]}`) continues on the next line and is not "
                 "statically resolvable - fails closed")
            problems += 1
            flagged.add("unterminated")
        # the dialect has no escapes: a backslash anywhere on a structural line is refused before any scan
        # reads the line (an escape can spell any key or value - `"u\x73es":` - and an escaped `\x7d` hides
        # an expression's end from the escape-blind pre-pass; the r6-r8 reviews). The tokenizer therefore
        # carries no escape model (a double-quoted node ends at its next `"`). Live workflows: 0 backslashes
        # on structural lines (a shell backslash lives in a `run: |` body, which is excised).
        if "\\" in ln and "backslash" not in flagged:
            fail(f"{name}: a backslash on a structural line (`{ln.strip()[:60]}`) is not statically resolvable - "
                 "a YAML escape can spell any key or value; write the value single-quoted when the backslash only "
                 "escaped a quote, and a literal backslash in a block scalar (`run: |`)")
            problems += 1
            flagged.add("backslash")
        # a quoted mapping key at ANY node start - block or flow position (the tokenizer's verdict): the dialect
        # reads plain keys only, so every reader matches ONE spelling - a quoted key had hidden `use-policy-store`
        # from the harden-runner read and `uses` from the engine-cache restore read (the r14 / r15 reviews), and a
        # line-leading arm had let a flow `concurrency: {…, "cancel-in-progress": true}` reach check (3)'s plain
        # read (the r16 review). 0 of the live structural lines carry one. A quoted ITEM (`- 'v*'`) and a quoted
        # VALUE are not keys and stay inside the shape.
        if _quoted_key(ln) and "quotedkey" not in flagged:
            fail(f"{name}: a quoted mapping key (`{ln.strip()[:60]}`, block or flow position) is refused - the dialect "
                 "reads plain keys only, so every reader matches one spelling (a quoted key hid `use-policy-store` "
                 "from the harden-runner read, `uses` from the engine-cache restore read and `cancel-in-progress` "
                 "from the concurrency read); write the key plainly")
            problems += 1
            flagged.add("quotedkey")
        b = blank_spans(ln)
        if re.search(node_start + r"<<\s*:", b) and "merge" not in flagged:
            fail(f"{name}: YAML merge key (<<:) is not statically verifiable - use plain block style")
            problems += 1
            flagged.add("merge")
        if re.search(node_start + r"&[^\s\[\]{},]", b) and "anchor" not in flagged:
            fail(f"{name}: YAML anchor (&name) is not statically verifiable - use plain block style")
            problems += 1
            flagged.add("anchor")
        if re.search(node_start + r"\*[^\s\[\]{},]", b) and "alias" not in flagged:
            fail(f"{name}: YAML alias (*name) is not statically verifiable - use plain block style")
            problems += 1
            flagged.add("alias")
        # a `!` at a value node-start (`: !`, `- !`, flow `[{,`) OR at the START of a line's
        # content (`^\s*!`, i.e. a tag on a mapping KEY like `  !!str packages: write`, which
        # a value-position-only scan would miss while the perms parser silently drops it).
        if re.search(node_start + r"!", b) and "tag" not in flagged:
            fail(f"{name}: an explicit YAML tag (!!type / !tag) changes how a value resolves and is "
                 "not statically verifiable - use a plain block value")
            problems += 1
            flagged.add("tag")
        # the line shape above refuses every indicator-led line (`?`, `&`, `*`, `!` at the start of a line's
        # content, after any dashes); what is left to these node-start scans is the VALUE position (`key: &a`,
        # `key: *a`, `key: !!str`), the flow position (`{ ? k : v }`, `[*a]`) and, for the merge key, the KEY
        # position (`<<:` at the start of a line or after a dash - a plain key the shape admits).
        if re.search(r"(?:^\s*(?:-\s+)*|[\[{,]\s*)\?\s", b) and "complexkey" not in flagged:
            fail(f"{name}: a YAML explicit/complex key (`? key`) is not statically verifiable - use a plain key")
            problems += 1
            flagged.add("complexkey")
        if _open_flow_depth(ln) and "flow" not in flagged:
            fail(f"{name}: a flow collection opened on one line and closed on another (`{ln.strip()[:60]}`) is "
                 "not statically verifiable - write it on one line or in block style")
            problems += 1
            flagged.add("flow")
        # the structural closure (the r7-r9 reviews): a step GitHub reads must be a block sequence item under
        # a block `steps:` under a block job under a block `jobs:`, so a `uses` key can only sit on a block
        # line and the (11) shape rule never reconstructs a flow item from YAML's quote / indicator grammar.
        # The line shape above refuses a flow collection at the start of a line's content (a sequence item
        # written as a flow collection - a step, a matrix `include:` entry, a dependabot list item - or a
        # flow value placed on the line after a bare `-` / `- # comment` / `steps:` / `jobs:` / a job id;
        # the r8 forms N1, N2, N6, G4) and a `?`-led line (the r9 form). Refused here: an inline value on
        # the top-level `jobs:` line (the r8 form G1 - parse_jobs reads only the block body, so an inline
        # `jobs: {...}` would skip every job-level check; a job-id line's inline value is refused in
        # parse_jobs) and a `steps` key followed by a flow collection anywhere on a line. A flow value
        # inline after any OTHER key (`branches: [main]`, `with: {path: x, key: k}`) is read as before.
        # Live workflows: 0 inline `jobs:` / job-id / `steps:` flow values.
        if re.match(r"^jobs\s*:\s*\S", ln) and "flowjobs" not in flagged:
            fail(f"{name}: an inline `jobs:` value (`{ln.strip()[:60]}`) is not statically resolvable - the "
                 "job-level checks read block jobs only; write the jobs in block style")
            problems += 1
            flagged.add("flowjobs")
        if re.search(r"(?<![\w-])steps\s*:\s*[\[{]", ln) and "flowsteps" not in flagged:
            fail(f"{name}: an inline flow `steps:` value (`{ln.strip()[:60]}`) is not statically resolvable - "
                 "write the steps in block style")
            problems += 1
            flagged.add("flowsteps")

    # duplicate top-level keys (YAML resolves to the LAST; a line scan trusts the FIRST)
    seen, dups = set(), set()
    for ln in lines:
        if not ln or ln[0].isspace() or ln.startswith(("---", "...")):
            continue
        if ":" not in ln:
            continue
        key = unquote(ln.split(":", 1)[0])
        if key in seen:
            dups.add(key)
        seen.add(key)
    for k in sorted(dups):
        fail(f"{name}: duplicate top-level key '{k}' (YAML resolves to the last; the gate cannot trust the first)")
        problems += 1

    return problems


def top_key(lines: list[str], key: str) -> tuple[str | None, list[str]]:
    """(inline value or None, indented body lines) for the top-level (col-0) `key:`.
    Quoted keys are normalised, so `'on':` == `on`. Duplicates are pre-rejected, so the
    first match is the only match."""
    inline, body, capturing = None, [], False
    for ln in lines:
        if not ln.strip():
            if capturing:
                body.append(ln)
            continue
        if not ln[0].isspace():
            if capturing:
                break
            head, _, val = ln.partition(":")
            if unquote(head) == key:
                capturing = True
                inline = val.strip() or None
            continue
        if capturing:
            body.append(ln)
    return inline, body


def first_level(body: list[str]) -> tuple[set[str], dict[str, list[str]]]:
    """(keys at the minimal indent, {key: deeper subtree lines}) - handles block-map
    (`push:`) and block-list (`- push`) forms."""
    real = [ln for ln in body if ln.strip()]
    if not real:
        return set(), {}
    base = min(len(ln) - len(ln.lstrip()) for ln in real)
    keys, sub, cur = set(), {}, None
    for ln in body:
        if not ln.strip():
            continue
        ind = len(ln) - len(ln.lstrip())
        if ind == base:
            s = ln.strip()
            name = s[2:] if s.startswith("- ") else s
            k = unquote(name.split(":", 1)[0].strip())
            keys.add(k)
            cur = k if not s.startswith("- ") else None
            if cur is not None:
                sub[cur] = []
        elif cur is not None and ind > base:
            sub[cur].append(ln)
    return keys, sub


def _item_content_indent(item: list[str]) -> int:
    """(pure) The content indent of a block-sequence item: the end of its `-` + whitespace prefix when a key
    follows on the dash line (any spacing - the r17 review), else - a bare `-`, which the shape admits - the indent
    of the next non-blank line, where YAML places the item's content at any deeper indent (the r18 review: a
    dash+2 guess had mis-read it); dash+2 only for an item with no content at all."""
    first = item[0]
    m = re.match(r"\s*-\s+(?=\S)", first)
    if m:
        return len(m.group(0))
    rest = [ln for ln in item[1:] if ln.strip()]
    return (len(rest[0]) - len(rest[0].lstrip())) if rest else first.index("-") + 2


def list_items(block: list[str]) -> list[list[str]]:
    """Split a YAML block sequence into per-item line groups: each `- ...` item, a bare `-` opening an item too
    (the shape admits it; the r18 review: a bare-dash step had merged into the previous step and lent a
    with-less harden step a decoy `egress-policy: block` input written under another action)."""
    items, cur, item_indent = [], None, None
    for ln in block:
        if not ln.strip():
            if cur is not None:
                cur.append(ln)
            continue
        indent = len(ln) - len(ln.lstrip())
        if re.match(r"-(?:\s|$)", ln.lstrip()):
            if item_indent is None:
                item_indent = indent
            if indent == item_indent:
                if cur is not None:
                    items.append(cur)
                cur = [ln]
                continue
        if cur is not None:
            cur.append(ln)
    if cur is not None:
        items.append(cur)
    return items


def item_children(item: list[str]) -> list[str]:
    """Direct child lines (at the item's content indent - the end of its `-` + whitespace prefix, ANY spacing,
    as step_with_block measures it) of a `- key: ...` sequence item, including the key inlined after the dash.
    A `-   key:` item puts its siblings at dash+4: a dash+2 assumption had hidden a step-level `if:` /
    `continue-on-error:` from the harden-runner own-key reads (the r17 review, fail-open against HEAD)."""
    if not item:
        return []
    first = item[0]
    content_indent = _item_content_indent(item)
    out = [" " * content_indent + first[content_indent:]] if len(first.rstrip()) > content_indent else []
    for ln in item[1:]:
        if not ln.strip():
            continue
        if (len(ln) - len(ln.lstrip())) == content_indent:
            out.append(ln)
    return out


def parse_jobs(jobs_body: list[str]) -> tuple[dict[str, list[str]], list[str]]:
    """(job-id -> DIRECT-child lines, the job ids written with an INLINE value). Direct children are
    the lines at the first deeper indent (step-level keys excluded). A job written inline - a
    flow mapping (`build: { ... }`), a flow sequence or any scalar on the job-id line - carries
    no block body a line reader can check, so its id is reported -> fail-closed (the structural
    closure's job-id arm; the top-level `jobs:` arm is the precheck's)."""
    real = [ln for ln in jobs_body if ln.strip()]
    if not real:
        return {}, []
    ji = min(len(ln) - len(ln.lstrip()) for ln in real)
    out: dict[str, list[str]] = {}
    inline: list[str] = []
    cur, child_indent, children = None, None, []
    for ln in jobs_body:
        if not ln.strip():
            continue
        indent = len(ln) - len(ln.lstrip())
        if indent == ji:
            head, _, val = ln.strip().partition(":")
            if val.strip():
                inline.append(unquote(head))
            if cur is not None:
                out[cur] = children
            cur, child_indent, children = unquote(head), None, []
        elif cur is not None and indent > ji:
            if child_indent is None:
                child_indent = indent
            if indent == child_indent:
                children.append(ln)
    if cur is not None:
        out[cur] = children
    return out, inline


def check_dependabot(root: Path) -> int:
    f = root / ".github" / "dependabot.yml"
    if not f.is_file():
        fail(f"{f} missing - G56 requires dependabot coverage")
        return 1
    text = f.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
    lines = structural_lines(text.split("\n"))
    problems = utf8_bom("dependabot.yml", text) + foreign_spaces("dependabot.yml", text) + precheck("dependabot.yml", lines)
    _, updates = top_key(lines, "updates")
    ecos: set[str] = set()
    for item in list_items(updates):
        # a flow-style item (`- { ... }`) cannot be field-verified by a line scan; reject
        # fail-closed with ONE clear message, consistent with the workflow flow-style rejections.
        head = item[0].lstrip()
        if head.startswith("- ") and head[2:].lstrip().startswith("{"):
            fm = re.search(r"package-ecosystem\s*:\s*['\"]?([A-Za-z][\w-]*)['\"]?\s*[,}]", "\n".join(item))
            fail(f"dependabot {('ecosystem ' + repr(fm.group(1))) if fm else 'item'} uses a flow-style "
                 "item - use block style so `package-ecosystem`/`directory`/`schedule` are verifiable")
            problems += 1
            continue
        children = item_children(item)
        ctext = "\n".join(children)
        # Resolve the ecosystem from the item's DIRECT-CHILD `package-ecosystem` KEY only
        # (line-anchored), NOT a substring of the whole item - so a decoy `package-ecosystem:`
        # planted in another field VALUE (e.g. `target-branch: "package-ecosystem: cargo"`)
        # cannot shadow the real key. Exactly one such key is required.
        # the value read to its END with balanced quotes - `"npm.x"` and `npm"` are not npm (the r13 / r14
        # reviews' unanchored-prefix and unbalanced-quote forms); an unreadable value is named as such, never
        # reported as a missing key; a quoted KEY never reaches this read (the dialect refuses it)
        eco_keys, unreadable = [], 0
        for val in re.findall(r"^\s*package-ecosystem\s*:\s*(.*?)\s*$", ctext, re.M):
            vm = re.fullmatch(r"(?P<q>['\"]?)(?P<v>[A-Za-z][\w-]*)(?P=q)", val)
            if vm is None:
                fail(f"dependabot `package-ecosystem` value {val!r} is not a plain or balanced-quoted name "
                     "(Dependabot cannot load such a config) - unreadable, fail-closed")
                problems += 1
                unreadable += 1
                continue
            eco_keys.append(vm.group("v"))
        if unreadable and not eco_keys:
            continue
        if len(eco_keys) != 1:
            fail(f"dependabot update item has {len(eco_keys)} direct-child `package-ecosystem` "
                 "keys (expected exactly 1)")
            problems += 1
            if not eco_keys:
                continue
        eco = eco_keys[0]
        ecos.add(eco)
        # defense-in-depth: a non-`package-ecosystem` field whose VALUE embeds the literal
        # `package-ecosystem:` is a coverage-spoof attempt - reject fail-closed.
        for cl in children:
            if not re.match(r"^\s*package-ecosystem\s*:", cl) and "package-ecosystem:" in cl:
                fail(f"dependabot ecosystem '{eco}': a field value embeds 'package-ecosystem:' "
                     "(coverage-spoof attempt)")
                problems += 1
                break
        if not re.search(r"^\s*director(y|ies)\s*:", ctext, re.M):
            fail(f"dependabot ecosystem '{eco}' block lacks a top-level `directory`")
            problems += 1
        if not re.search(r"^\s*schedule\s*:", ctext, re.M):
            fail(f"dependabot ecosystem '{eco}' block lacks a top-level `schedule`")
            problems += 1
    missing = REQUIRED_ECOSYSTEMS - ecos
    if missing:
        fail(f"dependabot.yml is missing ecosystem(s): {', '.join(sorted(missing))}")
        problems += 1
    return problems


def permission_blocks(lines: list[str]) -> list[tuple[int, list[str]]]:
    """(indent, body lines) for every BLOCK-form `permissions:` (top-level at indent 0
    and per-job at a deeper indent); inline / flow forms are handled separately."""
    out: list[tuple[int, list[str]]] = []
    i = 0
    while i < len(lines):
        m = re.match(r"^(\s*)permissions\s*:\s*(.*)$", lines[i])
        if m and m.group(2).strip() == "":  # block form (value on the following lines)
            indent = len(m.group(1))
            body, j = [], i + 1
            while j < len(lines):
                b = lines[j]
                if not b.strip():
                    j += 1
                    continue
                if (len(b) - len(b.lstrip())) > indent:
                    body.append(b)
                    j += 1
                else:
                    break
            out.append((indent, body))
            i = j
        else:
            i += 1
    return out


def report_perm_block(name: str, body: list[str], top_level: bool) -> int:
    """Assert a permissions block is read-only: no key set to write/write-all, no value
    that a line scan cannot resolve (empty/next-line or a block-scalar `|`/`>` indicator),
    and (top-level only) `contents: read`. id-token is reported once by the whole-file scan."""
    problems = 0
    perms: dict[str, str] = {}
    for ln in body:
        if not ln.strip():
            continue
        m = re.match(r"^\s+(\S+)\s*:\s*(.*)$", ln)
        if not m:
            # a non-blank permissions-body line the scalar-key:value pattern cannot parse
            # (e.g. a quoted key with an embedded space `"!!str packages": write`) must FAIL
            # closed, never be silently skipped.
            fail(f"{name}: unparseable permissions line {ln.strip()!r} - cannot prove read-only")
            problems += 1
            continue
        key, val = unquote(m.group(1)), m.group(2).strip()
        if val == "" or re.fullmatch(r"[|>][+\-0-9]*", val) or val[:1] in ("[", "{"):
            fail(f"{name}: permission '{key}' has a non-inline / block-scalar / flow value - cannot "
                 f"prove read-only (use `{key}: read`)")
            problems += 1
            perms[key] = "<unknown>"
        else:
            perms[key] = unquote(val)
    if top_level and perms.get("contents") != "read":
        fail(f"{name}: top-level permissions.contents is {perms.get('contents')!r}, must be read")
        problems += 1
    bad = sorted(k for k, v in perms.items() if v in ("write", "write-all") and k != "id-token")
    if bad:
        scope = "top-level" if top_level else "per-job"
        fail(f"{name}: {scope} permissions grants write: {', '.join(bad)} "
             "(read-only required; no job may elevate to write until the P10 release job)")
        problems += 1
    return problems


def job_bodies(jobs_body: list[str]) -> dict[str, list[str]]:
    """job-id -> ALL of the job's body lines (every depth below the header), so a scan can
    reach runs-on (a direct child) AND the steps (deeper) for secret refs / harden-runner."""
    real = [ln for ln in jobs_body if ln.strip()]
    if not real:
        return {}
    ji = min(len(ln) - len(ln.lstrip()) for ln in real)
    out: dict[str, list[str]] = {}
    cur, body = None, []
    for ln in jobs_body:
        if not ln.strip():
            continue
        ind = len(ln) - len(ln.lstrip())
        if ind == ji:
            if cur is not None:
                out[cur] = body
            cur, body = unquote(ln.strip().partition(":")[0]), []
        elif cur is not None and ind > ji:
            body.append(ln)
    if cur is not None:
        out[cur] = body
    return out


def child_block(body: list[str], key: str) -> list[str]:
    """The lines under the FIRST `key:` at body's direct-child indent (e.g. a job's
    `steps:` sequence)."""
    real = [ln for ln in body if ln.strip()]
    if not real:
        return []
    ci = min(len(ln) - len(ln.lstrip()) for ln in real)
    out, capturing = [], False
    for ln in body:
        if not ln.strip():
            if capturing:
                out.append(ln)
            continue
        ind = len(ln) - len(ln.lstrip())
        if ind == ci:
            if capturing:
                break
            if re.match(rf"^\s*{key}\s*:", ln):
                capturing = True
            continue
        if capturing:
            out.append(ln)
    return out


def job_direct_child_value(body: list[str], key: str) -> str | None:
    """The inline value of `key:` at the job's DIRECT-CHILD indent (so a deeper step-level
    `with: runs-on: <hosted>` cannot shadow a job-level `runs-on: self-hosted`)."""
    real = [ln for ln in body if ln.strip()]
    if not real:
        return None
    ci = min(len(ln) - len(ln.lstrip()) for ln in real)
    for ln in body:
        if not ln.strip() or (len(ln) - len(ln.lstrip())) != ci:
            continue
        m = re.match(rf"^\s*{key}\s*:\s*(\S.*)$", ln)
        if m:
            return m.group(1).strip()
    return None


def step_with_block(step: list[str]) -> list[str]:
    """The lines under a step item's own `with:` key (egress-policy must be a direct child
    of THIS step's with: mapping, not an env var / bare key / sibling step). The step's
    content indent is the end of the `-` + whitespace prefix (any spacing), or a bare dash's next line; a
    flow/inline `with: {...}` yields [] -> fail-closed."""
    if not step:
        return []
    first = step[0]
    ci = _item_content_indent(step)  # the end of the `- ` prefix (any spacing), or a bare dash's next line
    norm = ([" " * ci + first[ci:]] if len(first.rstrip()) > ci else []) + list(step[1:])
    out, capturing = [], False
    for ln in norm:
        if not ln.strip():
            if capturing:
                out.append(ln)
            continue
        ind = len(ln) - len(ln.lstrip())
        if ind == ci:
            if capturing:
                break
            if re.match(r"^\s*with\s*:\s*$", ln):  # block-form with: only (a flow `with: {..}` -> no body -> fail-closed)
                capturing = True
            continue
        if capturing and ind > ci:
            out.append(ln)
    return out


def job_has_harden_block(body: list[str]) -> bool:
    """True iff a STEP in this job is an UNCONDITIONAL step-security/harden-runner with
    `egress-policy: block` AS A DIRECT CHILD OF THAT STEP'S OWN `with:` MAPPING - so a decoy
    in a run-string / step `name:` / an `env:` var named egress-policy / a sibling step, an
    `if:`-gated step, a flow-style `with:`, `egress-policy: audit`, a value with a suffix, another case
    or an unbalanced quote (`block-x` / `Block` / `block"`) or a step carrying `use-policy-store` /
    `policy:` / `denied-endpoints` (the StepSecurity dashboard policy replaces the pinned mode - `Defaulting
    to audit mode.` in harden-runner's src/setup.ts; the v2.21.0 deny-list mode replaces block-with-allow-list)
    does NOT satisfy it; a PRESENT `continue-on-error` counts only when provably false (a block-scalar or
    expression value disqualifies). `uses` / `if` / `continue-on-error` are read from the step's OWN keys
    (item_children, at the step's real content indent whatever the dash spacing - the r17 review), so a decoy
    under `with:` / `env:` or the two lines written as inputs of another action do not stand in (the r16
    review); a duplicate `continue-on-error` key (YAML rejects it) is read line by line, fail-closed; a bare `-`
    opens a step of its own (the r18 review: a bare-dash sibling had merged into the harden step and lent it a
    decoy `egress-policy: block` input written under actions/checkout). A quoted key never reaches these reads:
    the dialect refuses it."""
    for step in list_items(child_block(body, "steps")):
        # the step's OWN keys only (the dash line's key and its siblings at the content indent, item_children):
        # a `continue-on-error: false` decoy under `with:` / `env:` cannot stand in for a step-level `true`, and
        # `uses: step-security/harden-runner` + `egress-policy: block` written as INPUTS of another action is no
        # harden step (the r16 review: a first-match read over the whole item had regressed against HEAD)
        own = "\n".join(item_children(step))
        # the EXACT action name only (a `(?![\w./-])` boundary, so a typosquat/fork like
        # `harden-runner-fork` or `harden-runner.evil` does NOT satisfy the requirement).
        if not re.search(r"^\s*uses\s*:\s*['\"]?step-security/harden-runner(?![\w./-])", own, re.M):
            continue
        if re.search(r"^\s*if\s*:", own, re.M):
            continue  # conditionally-run -> cannot prove it enforces
        coes = re.findall(r"^\s*continue-on-error\s*:\s*(.*)$", own, re.M)
        if any(not re.fullmatch(r"['\"]?(?:false|no|off|n)['\"]?\s*", v, re.I) for v in coes):
            continue  # a PRESENT continue-on-error counts only when EVERY such line is provably false: a truthy
            #          literal, a `${{ }}` expression or a block scalar the structural view excises (`|-` + an
            #          expression on the next line - the r15 review's pre-existing form) could tolerate a failing
            #          harden setup -> not unconditional enforcement (check (3)'s release polarity, fail-closed)
        with_body = step_with_block(step)
        if not with_body:
            continue
        wb = "\n".join(with_body)
        # `use-policy-store` (with or without an `api-key`) makes harden-runner fetch the StepSecurity dashboard
        # policy and ignore the pinned `egress-policy` (`Defaulting to audit mode.` in src/setup.ts at both v2.19.4
        # and v2.21.1); a `policy:` input names a stored policy the same way; `denied-endpoints` (v2.21.0) switches
        # to a deny-list mode that allows all other egress whenever `allowed-endpoints` is empty and the TLS probe
        # defaults to enabled (src/setup.ts, src/tls-inspect.ts). None is statically verifiable, so the step does
        # not count as BLOCK enforcement whatever its `egress-policy` line says (the r13 / r14 reviews' pre-existing
        # forms). A quoted key never reaches this read: the dialect refuses it (the r14 / r15 reviews). The key
        # is matched case-insensitively, as the runner matches input names.
        if re.search(r"^\s*(?:use-policy-store|policy|denied-endpoints)\s*:", wb, re.M | re.I):
            continue
        # the value is read to its END, balanced-quoted, case-SENSITIVE: `block-x`, `Block` and `block"` are not
        # `block` to harden-runner, which setFailed()s the step (the r13 / r14 reviews' unanchored-prefix forms; the
        # structural view has already cut a trailing comment). The key keeps its case-insensitive read (the runner
        # matches input names case-insensitively).
        if re.search(r"^\s*(?i:egress-policy)\s*:\s*(?:\"block\"|'block'|block)\s*$", wb, re.M):
            return True
    return False


def blank_quotes(s: str) -> str:
    """Replace single/double-quoted string spans with spaces (so a `}}`/`secrets` INSIDE a
    string literal - e.g. a `format('{{x}}', ...)` escape - is not read as structure)."""
    out, q = [], None
    for ch in s:
        if q:
            out.append(" ")
            if ch == q:
                q = None
        elif ch in "\"'":
            q = ch
            out.append(" ")
        else:
            out.append(ch)
    return "".join(out)


def reads_secret(text: str) -> bool:
    """True iff `text` references the GitHub `secrets` context inside any ${{ }} expression.
    Brace-and-quote aware: the terminating `}}` is the first one OUTSIDE a string literal
    (so a `format('{{lit}}', secrets.K)` escape does not end the expression early), and the
    `secrets` word is sought only OUTSIDE string literals in the interior (so a literal
    string `'secrets'` is not a false match). Spans expressions across physical lines."""
    i = 0
    while True:
        start = text.find("${{", i)
        if start < 0:
            return False
        j, q = start + 3, None
        while j < len(text):
            ch = text[j]
            if q:
                if ch == q:
                    q = None
            elif ch in "\"'":
                q = ch
            elif ch == "}" and j + 1 < len(text) and text[j + 1] == "}":
                break
            j += 1
        if SECRETS_WORD.search(blank_quotes(text[start + 3:j])):
            return True
        i = j + 2


def check_runner_host(name: str, lines: list[str], raw_lines: list[str]) -> int:
    """(6) Runner-host integrity (security-concept principle 11): a job that reads a CI
    secret must NEVER run on a self-hosted runner (it would share a host with the
    untrusted-corpus/fuzz jobs), and a GitHub-HOSTED secret job must carry an unconditional
    step-security/harden-runner step in BLOCK egress mode. A secret job whose runs-on is not
    a literal recognized hosted image (self-hosted / custom label / matrix / ${{ }} /
    object / list) is rejected fail-closed. Non-secret jobs are unconstrained (fail-open
    until the P10 signing job lands).

    Two views of the file are used to avoid a block-scalar working both ways: SECRET
    detection runs over `raw_lines` (comment-stripped, NOT block-scalar-excised) so a
    `${{ secrets.* }}` in a multi-line `run:` body / sibling `env:` is seen; STRUCTURAL
    analysis (runs-on, harden-runner step) runs over `lines` (block-scalar-EXCISED) so a
    `run: |` / `name: |` body whose text mimics `uses: harden-runner` / `egress-policy:
    block` cannot forge a harden step. Secrets are matched over the JOINED body so a
    `${{` and `secrets` split across physical lines are still caught."""
    problems = 0
    # a workflow-LEVEL env secret is injected into EVERY job, so it makes every job secret-reading.
    _, top_env_raw = top_key(raw_lines, "env")
    wf_env_secret = reads_secret("\n".join(top_env_raw))
    struct = job_bodies(top_key(lines, "jobs")[1])       # excised -> structure
    raw = job_bodies(top_key(raw_lines, "jobs")[1])       # non-excised -> secret scan
    for jid, body in struct.items():
        if not (wf_env_secret or reads_secret("\n".join(raw.get(jid, [])))):
            continue  # no secret read -> nothing to assert
        # Resolve the runs-on label(s) across scalar (`ubuntu-22.04`), flow-list (`[a, b]`),
        # and block-sequence (`runs-on:\n  - a\n  - b`) forms. A list/sequence runner must
        # carry EVERY listed label; a generic convention label (linux/x64/...) is only on
        # SELF-HOSTED runners, so a secret job must be a single literal hosted image OR an
        # all-hosted-image set - never a conjunction that pulls in a non-hosted label.
        runs_on = job_direct_child_value(body, "runs-on")
        if runs_on is not None:
            labels = ([unquote(e) for e in runs_on.strip("[]").split(",") if e.strip()]
                      if runs_on.startswith("[") else [unquote(runs_on)])
        else:  # block-sequence form (or unresolvable)
            labels = []
            for ln in child_block(body, "runs-on"):
                m = re.match(r"^\s*-\s*(\S.*)$", ln)
                if m:
                    labels.append(unquote(m.group(1).strip()))
        if not labels:
            fail(f"{name}: secret-reading job '{jid}' has no resolvable job-level `runs-on` - "
                 "cannot verify host (an object/empty runs-on is not statically verifiable)")
            problems += 1
            continue
        if any("self-hosted" in lbl.lower() for lbl in labels):
            fail(f"{name}: secret-reading job '{jid}' runs on a SELF-HOSTED runner - a CI secret must "
                 "never share a host with the untrusted-corpus/fuzz jobs (security-concept principle 11)")
            problems += 1
        elif labels and all(HOSTED_RUNNER.match(lbl) for lbl in labels):
            if not job_has_harden_block(body):
                fail(f"{name}: GitHub-hosted secret-reading job '{jid}' must carry an unconditional "
                     "step-security/harden-runner step with `egress-policy: block`")
                problems += 1
        else:
            fail(f"{name}: secret-reading job '{jid}' runs-on labels {labels!r} are not all recognized "
                 "GitHub-hosted images - a secret job must name a literal hosted runner / all-hosted set "
                 "(no matrix / expression / custom or convention label / object), so its host is "
                 "statically verifiable")
            problems += 1
    return problems


def check_fuzz_pin(name: str, raw_lines: list[str], fuzz_pin: str | None = None) -> int:
    """(7) cargo-fuzz toolchain pin (P0.2.10, G37/G56): every `cargo ... fuzz` invocation must
    name the DATE-PINNED nightly channel (`cargo +nightly-YYYY-MM-DD fuzz`), never a bare
    `+nightly` nor an implicit channel - cargo-fuzz's internal `+nightly` does NOT honour
    rust-toolchain.toml, so a fuzz run would silently float off the pinned toolchain that
    touches the to-be-minisigned bytes. Scans the comment-stripped (block-scalar-retained)
    text with shell backslash line-continuations collapsed, so a `cargo … fuzz` inside a
    `run: |` body - even split across continued lines - is seen. Fail-open until a fuzz job
    exists (P3+). EXACT-DATE cross-check (the named P0.4.3/P3 refinement, LIVE since P3.73
    landed the first fuzz invocation): when `fuzz_pin` is supplied (gate-tools.toml
    `[toolchain] fuzz_nightly`, read by main), the named channel must EQUAL it - a workflow
    pinning a DIFFERENT date is a drift between the committed pin and the actual run, exactly
    the float the pin exists to prevent. fuzz_pin=None (a fixture tree without gate-tools.toml)
    skips only the equality leg; the pin's own presence/format is check-fuzz-contract's job
    (no double-gating)."""
    problems = 0
    # collapse POSIX shell line-continuations (`\`<newline>) so a `cargo \`<NL>`fuzz` is seen as
    # one command (the regexes below would otherwise miss the split tokens).
    text = re.sub(r"\\\n\s*", " ", "\n".join(raw_lines))
    # SUBCOMMAND form: a `cargo … fuzz` where `fuzz` is a standalone subcommand token (NOT the
    # `fuzz` inside `cargo-fuzz`, via `(?<!-)`) and the cargo->fuzz segment stays within one
    # command (no `;`/`|`/`&`). Intervening global flags / `+channel` are tolerated; the segment
    # must carry a date-pinned `+nightly-YYYY-MM-DD` channel.
    for m in re.finditer(r"\bcargo\s+([^\n;|&]*?)(?<!-)\bfuzz\b", text):
        chan = re.search(r"\+(nightly-\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2})\b", m.group(1))
        if not chan:
            fail(f"{name}: `cargo … fuzz` must name the date-pinned nightly channel "
                 "(`cargo +nightly-YYYY-MM-DD fuzz`) - a bare / implicit / non-pinned +nightly "
                 "floats off the pinned toolchain (cargo-fuzz ignores rust-toolchain.toml)")
            problems += 1
        elif fuzz_pin is not None and chan.group(1) != fuzz_pin:
            fail(f"{name}: `cargo +{chan.group(1)} fuzz` names a channel != the committed pin "
                 f"{fuzz_pin!r} (gate-tools.toml [toolchain] fuzz_nightly) - the workflow and the "
                 "pin must move together (the P0.4.3 exact-date cross-check)")
            problems += 1
    # BINARY form `cargo-fuzz <subcmd>` AT A COMMAND POSITION - it cannot name a +channel so it
    # always floats. A command position is line-start, after a separator (;&|), a subshell/brace/
    # backtick opener, or `run:`, optionally preceded by env-assignments (`FOO=bar`) and command-
    # prefix words (time/nice/exec/command/sudo) or a loop/conditional keyword (do/then/else). An
    # install ARG (`cargo install … cargo-fuzz`) is NOT at a command position, so it is not flagged.
    # NB: the `|` in the boundary class also (intentionally) catches a cargo-fuzz on the first line
    # of a `run: |` block scalar (the YAML `|` indicator precedes it); keep it. The subcommand
    # regex above stays deliberately loose (it can over-reject a `--features fuzz`-style incidental
    # `fuzz` token - a SAFE over-fail) rather than tighten and risk missing an unpinned run.
    # The prefix set covers NO-ARG transparent command prefixes (env / nohup / setsid / doas /
    # time / nice / exec / command / sudo), inline env-assignments (`A=B`), and loop/conditional
    # keywords. ARG-BEARING prefixes (`timeout 60`, `nice -n 5`, `stdbuf -oL`, `taskset`/`ionice`),
    # forwarding programs (`xargs`), path-prefixed invocations (`./cargo-fuzz`, `/usr/bin/cargo-fuzz`),
    # and flag-bearing prefixes are the named P0.4.3/P3 precise-cargo-invocation-parse refinement
    # (enumeration is the wrong tool there); the gate is fail-open until a fuzz job lands (P3).
    _binbound = (r"(?:(?:^|\n)\s*|[;&|(){}`]\s*|run\s*:\s*)"
                 r"(?:\w+=\S*\s+|(?:time|nice|exec|command|sudo|env|nohup|setsid|doas|do|then|else|while|until|elif)\s+)*")
    for _ in re.finditer(_binbound + r"cargo-fuzz\b", text):
        fail(f"{name}: the `cargo-fuzz` binary form cannot pin a toolchain channel - use the "
             "subcommand form `cargo +nightly-YYYY-MM-DD fuzz` so the nightly is date-pinned")
        problems += 1
    return problems


# (8) build.rs / proc-macro execution-isolation for the secret-bearing job. A COMPILING command runs
# build scripts + proc-macros (arbitrary native code). That is (a) cargo's compiling subcommands -
# build/test/run/bench/check + install/doc/publish/package/clippy/rustc/miri (NOT fetch/metadata/tree/
# vendor/update, which do not compile); AND (b) the `tauri build` wrappers (which invoke `cargo build`):
# `cargo tauri build` (matched by alt 1), `pnpm/npx/yarn/bunx tauri build`, a bare `tauri build`, and the
# `tauri-apps/tauri-action` build step - so the "a compile in the secret-bearing job" anti-pattern is
# caught regardless of whether the spec's disjoint sign-only job (§6.7.2) is ever violated cargo- OR
# tauri-wrapper-side.
_COMPILE_RE = re.compile(
    r"\bcargo\s+(?:[^\n;|&]*\s)?(?:build|test|run|bench|check|install|doc|publish|package|clippy|rustc|miri)\b"
    r"|\b(?:pnpm|npx|yarn|bunx)\s+(?:[^\n;|&]*\s)?tauri\s+(?:[^\n;|&]*\s)?build\b"
    r"|(?:^|[\s;|&(){}`])tauri\s+build\b"
    r"|tauri-apps/tauri-action")
_CARGO_FETCH_LOCKED_RE = re.compile(r"\bcargo\s+fetch\b[^\n;|&]*--locked\b")
# CARGO_NET_OFFLINE truthy as an env (`CARGO_NET_OFFLINE: "true"` / `: 1`) or inline (`CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true cargo …`)
_NET_OFFLINE_TRUE_RE = re.compile(r"CARGO_NET_OFFLINE\s*[:=]\s*[\"']?(?:true|1)\b", re.I)


def check_secret_offline_build(name: str, lines: list[str], raw_lines: list[str]) -> int:
    """(8) build.rs/proc-macro execution-isolation (P0.4.4, §3.8): `build.rs` + proc-macros run
    arbitrary native code during `cargo build`/`test` in the SAME job that holds MINISIGN_SECRET_KEY,
    with full network by default, and `cargo-vet` is a TRUST signal not an execution sandbox - so the
    secret-bearing job must run cargo build/test with `CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true` AFTER an explicit
    `cargo fetch --locked` (the build then cannot phone home to exfiltrate the key). harden-runner BLOCK
    (rule 6) is the network backstop; this is the cargo-level no-network-after-fetch invariant. The full
    per-crate sandbox is the owner-decidable cargo-acl cap (P0.4.5). Fail-open until the P10 signing job
    lands (no secret-bearing build job exists yet). Secret detection mirrors rule (6) (raw_lines, so a
    secret in a multi-line run:/env: is seen)."""
    problems = 0
    _, top_env_raw = top_key(raw_lines, "env")
    wf_env_secret = reads_secret("\n".join(top_env_raw))
    struct = job_bodies(top_key(lines, "jobs")[1])
    raw = job_bodies(top_key(raw_lines, "jobs")[1])
    for jid in struct:
        body = raw.get(jid, [])
        if not (wf_env_secret or reads_secret("\n".join(body))):
            continue                                          # no secret read -> nothing to assert
        # collapse POSIX shell line-continuations so a `cargo \`<NL>`build` is one command (as rule 7).
        text = re.sub(r"\\\n\s*", " ", "\n".join(body))
        # CARGO_NET_OFFLINE may be set at the JOB-level env (before `steps:`), so scan the WHOLE body
        # for it. The COMPILE + FETCH scan is scoped to the `steps:` section so a `cargo build` embedded
        # in a job-level env VALUE / name: (a phantom match) is not mistaken for a real compile step.
        # (Honest P10 residual: this is line/text-shape, not per-step env-resolution - a step-level
        # `CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=false` inline override after a job-level `true` is NOT detected here; the
        # harden-runner BLOCK egress allow-list (check 6) is the live backstop, and the per-crate cap is
        # the P0.4.5 cargo-acl upgrade. A naive `=false`-reject would false-fail the legitimate
        # fetch-step-overrides-false form, so it is deliberately not added.)
        m_steps = re.search(r"(?m)^\s*steps\s*:", text)
        scan = text[m_steps.start():] if m_steps else text
        builds = list(_COMPILE_RE.finditer(scan))
        if not builds:
            continue                                          # no compile in this secret job's steps
        fetch = _CARGO_FETCH_LOCKED_RE.search(scan)
        if not fetch:
            fail(f"{name}: secret-reading job '{jid}' runs a compiling command (cargo build/test/… or "
                 "`tauri build`) but has no explicit `cargo fetch --locked` - a build script/proc-macro "
                 "could fetch-and-exfiltrate MINISIGN_SECRET_KEY (fetch online FIRST, then build offline)")
            problems += 1
        if not _NET_OFFLINE_TRUE_RE.search(text):
            fail(f"{name}: secret-reading job '{jid}' runs a compiling command without CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true "
                 "- a build script/proc-macro can phone home (set it after `cargo fetch --locked`)")
            problems += 1
        elif fetch and any(b.start() < fetch.start() for b in builds):
            fail(f"{name}: secret-reading job '{jid}' runs a compiling command BEFORE its `cargo fetch "
                 "--locked` - the pre-fetch must come first so every build runs offline")
            problems += 1
    return problems


# Requires a registry-qualified multi-segment name: a single-segment Docker Hub shorthand
# (`debian@sha256:...`) fails DELIBERATELY - the explicit `docker.io/library/...` spelling is
# the one whose resolution does not depend on the client's shorthand rules (a safe over-fail).
# A registry with an explicit PORT (`host:5000/img@sha256:...`) also over-fails (the host part
# admits no `:`); a repo image needing one lands as a deliberate widening of this regex then.
_IMAGE_DIGEST = re.compile(
    r"[a-z0-9.\-]+(?:/[A-Za-z0-9._\-]+)+(?::[A-Za-z0-9._\-]+)?@sha256:[0-9a-f]{64}"
)


def check_images_and_engine_cache(name: str, lines: list[str], raw_lines: list[str]) -> int:
    """(9) image digests + (10) the G37 engine-cache restore/stage-engines ordering.

    Two views, the check-(8) precedent: (9) scans the block-scalar-EXCISED structural lines
    (an `image:`-shaped string inside a `run:` body must not false-fire), while (10) scans the
    RAW (comment-stripped, NOT excised) job bodies - a `run: |` block is exactly where a
    `scripts/stage-engines` invocation lives, so the excised view would be blind to the one
    shape the repo actually writes."""
    problems = 0
    # (9) every `container:` scalar / `image:` value must be digest-pinned; a value this scan
    # cannot resolve fails closed - including the empty/next-line form (the rule-(5) precedent).
    for i, ln in enumerate(lines):
        bare = re.match(r"^(\s*)(image|container)\s*:\s*$", ln)
        if bare:
            indent, key = len(bare.group(1)), bare.group(2)
            if key == "image":
                fail(f"{name}: `image:` with no value on the key's line - a next-line/block "
                     "value is not statically resolvable, so it fails closed (G56 image-digest "
                     "sub-rule)")
                problems += 1
            else:
                # A bare `container:` is the legitimate block-opener IFF its child block carries
                # an `image:` key (which the scans here then resolve); a child block WITHOUT one
                # is a next-line scalar or an imageless mapping - unresolvable, fails closed.
                child_has_image = False
                for nxt in lines[i + 1:]:
                    if not nxt.strip():
                        continue
                    if len(nxt) - len(nxt.lstrip()) <= indent:
                        break
                    if re.match(r"^\s*image\s*:", nxt):
                        child_has_image = True
                        break
                if not child_has_image:
                    fail(f"{name}: `container:` block with no resolvable `image:` child - "
                         "unresolvable by this scan, so it fails closed (G56 image-digest "
                         "sub-rule)")
                    problems += 1
            continue
        m = re.match(r"^\s*(image|container)\s*:\s*(\S.*)$", ln)
        if not m:
            continue
        value = unquote(m.group(2).strip())
        if not _IMAGE_DIGEST.fullmatch(value):
            fail(f"{name}: `{m.group(1)}: {value}` is not a digest-pinned "
                 "`<registry>/<image>[:tag]@sha256:<64-hex>` image (the G56 image-digest "
                 "sub-rule - a mutable tag, expression or flow form fails closed)")
            problems += 1
    # (10) a job restoring an engine-asset cache may not also run scripts/stage-engines: no
    # lint-recognizable G37 re-verify actor exists between them, so the pair fails closed
    # until the owner act P10.62 extends this rule with the verify step. Job
    # identity comes from the structural view; every content predicate runs over the RAW body.
    raw_jobs = job_bodies(top_key(raw_lines, "jobs")[1])
    for jid in job_bodies(top_key(lines, "jobs")[1]):
        joined = "\n".join(raw_jobs.get(jid, []))
        restores_engine_cache = (
            re.search(r"uses\s*:\s*['\"]?actions/cache", joined)
            and ".engine-cache" in joined
        )
        if restores_engine_cache and "scripts/stage-engines" in joined:
            fail(f"{name}: job '{jid}' restores an engine-asset cache AND invokes "
                 "`scripts/stage-engines` - the G37 restore-re-verify actor between them is "
                 "not lint-recognizable, so the pair is refused; the owner act P10.62 "
                 "extends this rule with the sanctioned verify step")
            problems += 1
    return problems


def check_workflow(path: Path, fuzz_pin: str | None = None) -> int:
    name = path.name
    text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
    raw = text.split("\n")
    lines = structural_lines(raw)
    # check (6) takes BOTH views: the excised `lines` for STRUCTURE (runs-on / harden step,
    # so a block-scalar body cannot forge a harden step) and the comment-stripped-but-NOT-
    # excised `stripped` for SECRET detection (so a secret in a multi-line `run:` body or a
    # sibling `env:` is still seen). checks (1)-(5) use the excised `lines`.
    stripped = [strip_comment(ln) for ln in raw]
    problems = utf8_bom(name, text) + foreign_spaces(name, text) + precheck(name, lines)
    problems += check_runner_host(name, lines, stripped)
    problems += check_fuzz_pin(name, stripped, fuzz_pin)
    problems += check_secret_offline_build(name, lines, stripped)
    problems += check_images_and_engine_cache(name, lines, stripped)

    # (5a) flow-style permissions anywhere (top-level OR per-job) cannot be statically
    # verified for contents-read / id-token - reject fail-closed. Empty `{}`/`{ }`
    # (= no permissions, most restrictive) is allowed.
    if any(re.match(r"^\s*permissions\s*:\s*\{\s*[^}\s]", ln) for ln in lines):
        fail(f"{name}: flow-style `permissions: {{...}}` is unsupported - use block style "
             "(so contents-read and the id-token prohibition are verifiable)")
        problems += 1

    # (5b) inline-SCALAR permissions (top-level OR per-job; GitHub per-job permissions
    # OVERRIDE the read-only top-level default): the only read-only inline scalar GitHub
    # accepts is `read-all` (empty `{}` flow is handled by (5a)); write-all / write / any
    # other scalar is rejected fail-closed.
    for ln in lines:
        m = re.match(r"^\s*permissions\s*:\s*([^\s{].*)$", ln)
        if m and unquote(m.group(1).strip()) != "read-all":
            fail(f"{name}: inline `permissions: {unquote(m.group(1).strip())}` is not read-only "
                 "(use block `contents: read`, or `read-all`)")
            problems += 1

    # (5c) every BLOCK-form permissions (top-level + per-job): no write key, no value a
    # line scan cannot resolve; contents: read required on the top-level block only. An
    # empty/null body (`permissions:` with nothing under it) is left to (5d).
    for indent, pbody in permission_blocks(lines):
        if not pbody:
            continue
        problems += report_perm_block(name, pbody, top_level=(indent == 0))

    # (5d) a top-level permissions context MUST exist (the inline-scalar read-only check is (5b)).
    p_inline, p_body = top_key(lines, "permissions")
    if p_inline is None and not p_body:
        fail(f"{name}: no top-level `permissions:` block (want contents: read)")
        problems += 1

    # (2) id-token discipline - any id-token permission key (top-level or per-job), in
    # block or quoted-key form. A flow-style `permissions: { id-token: write }` is caught
    # by (5a); a `run:`-string mention of "id-token" never starts a line, so no false-positive.
    if any(re.match(r"^\s*id-token\s*:", ln) for ln in lines):
        fail(f"{name}: an `id-token:` permission key is set - not allowed until the P10 attestation job")
        problems += 1

    # (3) concurrency polarity - depth-aware trigger classification.
    # A flow-MAPPING value under on: (`on: {...}` or `push: { tags: ... }`) hides the
    # trigger structure from a line scan, so it is unclassifiable -> fail-closed (mirroring
    # the flow-jobs / flow-permissions rejections). A flow SEQUENCE (`branches: [main]`,
    # `on: [push, pull_request]`) is fine - it carries no hidden keys.
    on_inline, on_body = top_key(lines, "on")
    events, sub = first_level(on_body)
    # Only at the FIRST indent level under on: (the event-name level) does a flow-MAPPING
    # value (`push: { tags }`) or a block-list-of-MAPPINGS item (`- push:`, which GitHub
    # does not accept) hide the trigger structure -> unclassifiable -> fail-closed. A flow
    # SEQUENCE (`branches: [main]`) and a DEEPER `- cron:` under schedule: (the canonical
    # valid scheduled form) carry no hidden trigger and stay classifiable.
    unparseable_on = False
    _on_real = [ln for ln in on_body if ln.strip()]
    if _on_real:
        _base = min(len(ln) - len(ln.lstrip()) for ln in _on_real)
        for ln in _on_real:
            if (len(ln) - len(ln.lstrip())) != _base:
                continue
            if re.search(r":\s*\{", ln) or re.match(r"^\s*-\s+\S+\s*:", ln):
                unparseable_on = True
                break
    if on_inline is not None:
        v = on_inline.strip()
        if v.startswith("["):
            events |= {unquote(x) for x in v.strip("[]").split(",") if x.strip()}
        elif v.startswith("{"):
            unparseable_on = True
        elif re.fullmatch(r"[a-z_]+", unquote(v)):
            events.add(unquote(v))
        else:
            unparseable_on = True

    c_inline, c_body = top_key(lines, "concurrency")
    conc_text = (c_inline or "") + "\n" + "\n".join(c_body)
    # YAML-1.1 truthy spellings (GitHub Actions resolves True/TRUE/yes/on like snakeyaml);
    # tolerate an explicit !!tag prefix and quotes (an actual tag is already pre-rejected).
    # PUSH side needs cancel = a YAML-1.1 truthy spelling. RELEASE side is fully fail-closed:
    # a cancel-in-progress that is PRESENT and not PROVABLY falsy (false/no/off) must fail -
    # so an exotic-but-truthy spelling, an unresolvable ${{ }} expression, or a multi-line
    # value cannot smuggle a release-cancelling config past the gate.
    has_cancel_truthy = bool(
        re.search(r'cancel-in-progress\s*:\s*(?:!!\S+\s+)?["\']?(?:true|yes|on|y)\b', conc_text, re.I))
    cancel_present = bool(re.search(r'cancel-in-progress\s*:', conc_text))
    cancel_falsy = bool(
        re.search(r'cancel-in-progress\s*:\s*(?:!!\S+\s+)?["\']?(?:false|no|off|n)\b', conc_text, re.I))
    if unparseable_on:
        fail(f"{name}: on: trigger is not statically classifiable (flow-style / alias / unknown form) "
             "- use plain block style so concurrency polarity is verifiable")
        problems += 1
    else:
        is_release_class = bool({"release", "create"} & events)
        if "push" in sub:
            push_keys, _ = first_level(sub["push"])
            if "tags" in push_keys:
                is_release_class = True
        # release safety dominates: a workflow that is BOTH push-branch and release-class is
        # classified release-class (only the must-NOT-cancel rule applies), never both.
        is_push_branch = ("push" in events) and not is_release_class
        if is_push_branch and not has_cancel_truthy:
            fail(f"{name}: push-triggered workflow lacks `concurrency.cancel-in-progress: true`")
            problems += 1
        if is_release_class and cancel_present and not cancel_falsy:
            fail(f"{name}: release-class workflow must NOT set cancel-in-progress "
                 "(a cancelled release is worse than a delayed one; only an explicit "
                 "false/no/off passes - an expression or exotic value fails closed)")
            problems += 1

    # (4) per-JOB timeout-minutes; a job written inline (flow-style or any scalar) fails closed; uses:-jobs exempt
    _, jobs_body = top_key(lines, "jobs")
    jobs, inline_jobs = parse_jobs(jobs_body)
    if inline_jobs:
        fail(f"{name}: job(s) {inline_jobs} written flow-style / inline (`build: {{ ... }}` or any value on the "
             "job-id line) are unsupported - use block style so the job-level checks (timeout-minutes, "
             "runs-on, permissions, steps) are verifiable")
        problems += 1
    else:
        for jid, children in jobs.items():
            # exempt a reusable-workflow CALL job (GitHub forbids job-level timeout-minutes
            # there) - but ONLY when `uses:` names a real reusable workflow under
            # `.github/workflows/` (local `./.github/workflows/x.yml` or cross-repo
            # `org/repo/.github/workflows/x.yml@ref`), so a bogus `uses:` value buys nothing.
            if any(re.match(r"^\s*uses\s*:", c) and re.search(r"\.github/workflows/", c) for c in children):
                continue
            if not any(re.match(r"^\s*timeout-minutes\s*:", c) for c in children):
                fail(f"{name}: job '{jid}' lacks a job-level `timeout-minutes`")
                problems += 1
    return problems


# (11) the `uses` SHAPE (the r2-r4 review class: a shape rule, not a list of odd forms). The one form this
# reader resolves is a block step line `[- ]uses: <value>` - the value on the same line, no block-scalar
# indicator, the key plain and the value plainly quoted at most; every other occurrence of a `uses` key token on a
# structural line fails closed. The rule is sound under the dialect the precheck enforces (every structural
# line a `key:` entry or a `- ` item, so a key and its `:` share a line; no backslash; no inline `jobs:` /
# job-id / `steps:` flow value; no spanning flow; no anchor / alias / merge key / tag / flow-position explicit
# key; no quoted node running off its line; no BOM; no character Python treats as white space or a line break
# outside the four every YAML parser agrees on, lines split at LF only): a step GitHub reads then
# sits on a block line, read by the one tokenizer (_tokenize) the structural comment cut, the quote blanking,
# the flow depth and the precheck share.
_USES_KEY_RE = re.compile(r"(?<![\w-])uses\s*:")
_USES_STEP_RE = re.compile(r"^\s*(?:-\s+)?uses\s*:\s*(.*)$")


_CACHE_NAME_RE = re.compile(r"(?i)(?<![\w./-])actions/cache(?:/save|/restore)?(?![\w-])")
_CACHE_PIN_RE = re.compile(r"(?i:actions/cache(?:/save|/restore)?)@([0-9a-f]{40})")


def check_cache_lockstep(wfs: list[Path]) -> int:
    """(11) every `actions/cache`, `actions/cache/save` and `actions/cache/restore` `uses:` across ALL the
    given workflows pins ONE lowercase 40-hex sha: one action version writes AND reads a cache entry.
    Dependabot offers the halves as two PRs (the 2026-09-07 #30 precedent: save only), and a split would
    save under one action version and restore under another. Read over the STRUCTURAL view (comment-
    stripped, block-scalar bodies excised: a `run: |` body is a string GitHub reads no step from). The
    `uses` SHAPE rule (_USES_KEY_RE / _USES_STEP_RE): every `uses` key token must be the key of a plain
    block step line with its value on the same line and no block-scalar indicator - a next-line or
    block-scalar value, a second key token on the line and a `uses` key in any other position fail
    CLOSED. The rule is sound under the
    dialect precheck enforces (the r7-r9 structural closure): every structural line is a `key:` entry or
    a `- ` item (a key and its `:` share a line; no `?`- or `:`-led line, no continuation line, no flow
    collection at a line start), no backslash on a structural line (so no escape spells a key and the
    tokenizer needs no escape model), no inline `jobs:` / job-id / `steps:` flow value, no flow
    collection spanning lines, no quoted node running off its line, no flow-position explicit key, no
    anchor or alias of any YAML name, no merge key, no tag, no BOM, no character Python treats as white
    space or a line break outside the four every YAML parser agrees on, lines split at LF only - so a
    step GitHub reads is a block sequence item and its `uses` key lives on a block line; and
    structural_lines bounds a block scalar by its header's KEY column (a folded `- name: >-` never
    swallows the step's `uses:`; the r5/r6 reviews) - over the SAME node-aware view this rule reads (one
    tokenizer for the structural comment cut, the quote blanking, the unterminated scan and the flow
    depth, so a quote inside a plain scalar opens nothing anywhere; the raw view of checks (6), (8),
    (10) keeps the shell-quote-aware cut). A plainly quoted value is read through its quotes, a quoted key is refused;
    the owner/repo is matched case-insensitively and token-bounded (a local
    `./.github/actions/cache-engines` is not the cache action); a resolved cache `uses:` whose sha is not
    lowercase 40-hex fails closed."""
    shas: dict[str, set[str]] = {}
    problems = 0
    for wf in wfs:
        for ln in structural_lines(wf.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").split("\n")):
            keys = _USES_KEY_RE.findall(ln)
            if not keys:
                continue
            m = _USES_STEP_RE.match(ln)
            value = m.group(1).strip() if m else ""
            if m is None or len(keys) != 1 or not value or value[0] in "|>":
                fail(f"{wf.name}: `uses` outside the one shape this reader resolves (`- uses: <value>` on one line): "
                     f"`{ln.strip()[:60]}` - a next-line or block-scalar value, a second key token or a `uses` key "
                     "in any other position fails closed (G56 cache-lockstep sub-rule)")
                problems += 1
                continue
            if not _CACHE_NAME_RE.search(value):
                continue
            pin = _CACHE_PIN_RE.fullmatch(unquote(value))
            if pin is None:
                fail(f"{wf.name}: `uses: {value}` names actions/cache without a lowercase 40-hex sha pin - "
                     "fails closed (G56 cache-lockstep sub-rule)")
                problems += 1
                continue
            shas.setdefault(pin.group(1).lower(), set()).add(wf.name)
    if len(shas) > 1:
        where = "; ".join(f"{sha[:12]}… in {sorted(files)}" for sha, files in sorted(shas.items()))
        fail(f"actions/cache halves are not in lockstep - {where} (save and restore must pin ONE version; "
             "the G56 cache-lockstep sub-rule)")
        problems += 1
    return problems


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description="G56 CI supply-chain config hygiene (P0.2.6)")
    ap.add_argument("--root", type=Path,
                    default=Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent,
                    help="repo root to check (default: the repo; a fixture tree in the self-test)")
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)

    gh = args.root / ".github"
    if not gh.is_dir():
        print(f"[G56] .github not found under {args.root}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    problems = check_dependabot(args.root)

    # the committed fuzz-nightly pin for the check-(7) exact-date cross-check (LIVE since P3.73).
    # Absent/malformed gate-tools.toml -> None (a self-test fixture tree; the pin's own presence/
    # format is check-fuzz-contract's gate - no double-gating here).
    fuzz_pin: str | None = None
    gt = args.root / "scripts" / "gate-tools.toml"
    if gt.is_file():
        try:
            fuzz_pin = tomllib.loads(gt.read_text(encoding="utf-8")).get("toolchain", {}).get("fuzz_nightly")
        except (tomllib.TOMLDecodeError, UnicodeDecodeError, OSError):
            fuzz_pin = None

    wfdir = gh / "workflows"
    wfs = (sorted(wfdir.glob("*.yml")) + sorted(wfdir.glob("*.yaml"))) if wfdir.is_dir() else []
    if not wfs:
        fail("no workflow files under .github/workflows/")
        problems += 1
    for wf in wfs:
        problems += check_workflow(wf, fuzz_pin)
    problems += check_cache_lockstep(wfs)     # (11) cross-workflow: one sha for every actions/cache half

    if problems:
        print(f"[G56] CI supply-chain hygiene FAILED ({problems} problem(s)).", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    print(f"[G56] CI supply-chain hygiene OK ({len(wfs)} workflow(s) + dependabot.yml)")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
